  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper uses SAE features and a feature-entanglement premise to build a T2I concept-erasure method, and reports erasure and fidelity results. It does not test the listed toy-network, capacity-account, LLM, SAE-canonicality, or feature-dimensionality claims.",
    "gate_note": "The paper measures activation shifts after zero-ablating sensitive SAE features and reports outcomes for feature-suppression strategies. These are results about T2I concept erasure and collateral effects, not direct tests of any listed claim version. Broad baseline comparisons and layer-selection results are not included as claim rows.",
    "test_reason": "The study uses SAE features from the Stable Diffusion 1.4 text encoder as a tool for concept erasure and reports only its method's erasure and fidelity results. It runs no toy models and studies no LLMs. It does not test SAE completeness or canonicality, capacity-driven polysemanticity, or feature dimensionality. Superposition and entanglement are stated as premises, not tested.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that SAE localization plus null-space projection against coupled benign features gives a better safety–fidelity trade-off than baselines. They acknowledge dependence on SAE resolution, capacity and reconstruction fidelity.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Uses a trained Top-K SAE and activation-space null-space projection for concept erasure in text-to-image diffusion models; it is not the original toy-network superposition experiment.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The authors report that the intervention layer affects I2P detections and that λ below 3 leaves residual harmful content while values above 3 cause semantic drift. These are method-performance sensitivities, not tests of the listed claim versions.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "In SD1.4's text encoder, subtracting the decoder directions of top sensitive SAE features and re-encoding produces large, long-tailed activation shifts in a subset of other SAE features. Remaining features stay near zero (Fig. 7, qualitative only). The coupled set is selected as the top-k by shift, so the split is partly by construction.",
        "proposed_claim": "SAE dictionary directions are non-orthogonal, so ablating some features shifts the encoder's readout of others; this coupling concentrates in a minority of features.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r2",
          "r3"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "preprint",
    "problems": "No uncertainty estimates are reported for the relevant measurements. The paper states that its geometric guarantee is a local linear approximation and depends on SAE completeness, dictionary capacity, and reconstruction fidelity; its experiments are limited to T2I models and concept-erasure outcomes.",
    "rows_left_out": "Left out broad baseline comparisons in Tables 1 and 5, layer-selection results in Table 2, random-neuron suppression and activation amplification results, and general cross-model safety results: these report concept-erasure performance but do not test a listed claim version or directly measure the feature-coupling finding cataloged here.",
    "untested": [
